## The problem

A retirement removes the quantities $E$, the terms $\mathcal T_E$ joined to them, and the mapped
values reached from $E$ in the compilation graph, as the core's
[retirement](../core/math/#retirement) says. Every other node stays. That rule is safe, but it
leaves things behind. A constant is reached from no quantity, so it is never removed, even after
the last term that read it is gone. The same holds for a value folded from constants. In a
sliding window, every tick adds scales, anchors and other constants. Each replacement adds a
matrix and a right-hand side. The replacement is retired in its turn, and all of those constants
stay. In the loop of the `sliding_window` example the model held 854 nodes after tick 9 and 4,121
after tick 79, while its quantities and terms stayed between 33 and 50. The cost is memory, not
time, since a program is compiled only from what its targets reach. But it grows without bound,
so a model that runs indefinitely is not supported.

This entry works out which nodes a retirement should also remove: those it leaves needed by
nothing. It shows that removing them changes nothing that a query, a proposal or a role reads, and
that one walk from the retirement's removals finds them.

## Setting

The objects are the core's, in [its mathematics](../core/math/). The
[compilation graph](../core/math/#the-compilation-graph) has the model's values as nodes:
quantities, constants and mapped values. It has an edge from each input of an operation to the
operation's output. It is acyclic, and its roots are the quantities and the constants. A node's
*consumers* are the nodes its outgoing edges lead to, and its *ancestors* are the nodes it is
reached from. A node is *fixed* when it is a constant or is reached from no quantity. The core
folds every operation whose inputs are all fixed, so a fixed mapped node has its value in the
model.

The compilation graph's edges are not the only things that refer to a node. The model also holds
nodes by name, outside those edges:

- each term's log-density $\logterm_t$;
- each node bound to a role of a term, which the term reads through the role and need not consume
  (a normal's scale, bound to its role, is read by estimators as well as consumed by the density);
- each proposal's inputs and its log-density.

Write $H(M)$ for the nodes a model $M$ holds this way. Some other set of nodes may also be kept
alive deliberately. Call it $P$, the *pinned* nodes. Whether $P$ is empty, or which nodes it
holds, is the entry's open question
([Handles the user holds](#handles-the-user-holds)). The mathematics below holds for any $P$.
Write $K(M) = H(M) \cup P$ for what the model keeps.

## What a retirement leaves behind

The core's retirement removes the set

$$
	R = E \cup \{\, n : n \text{ is reached from } E \,\}, @tag(removed)
$$

which contains the log-density of every term of $\mathcal T_E$, since such a term is joined to a
quantity of $E$. It also drops references that are not edges: the role bindings of the terms of
$\mathcal T_E$, and the proposals it removes, those of $E$ and of the parameters whose proposal
read $E$. It then adds the replacement $\logterm_B$, built in the retirement's own scope.

Three kinds of node can lose their last use this way. Together they are the *candidates*:

$$
	C = \bigl(\operatorname{in}(R) \setminus R\bigr) \cup \bigl(H(M) \setminus H(M') \setminus R\bigr) \cup S, @tag(candidates)
$$

where $M$ and $M'$ are the model before and after the retirement, $\operatorname{in}(R)$ is the
inputs of the removed nodes, and $S$ is the nodes created in the retirement's scope. The first
part is the nodes that fed something removed. The second is the nodes the model held through a
dropped term or proposal. The third is everything the caller computed while building the
replacement, including intermediate values the replacement does not use.

A candidate need not be fixed. Take a retired term $\logterm_t(\point_0, g(\point_1))$, in which
$g(\point_1)$ is a mapped value computed from a blanket quantity alone. $g(\point_1)$ is not
reached from $E$, so it is not removed, and its only consumer is. Such a node is not reached by
the growth above, since it is removed when $\point_1$ itself is retired. But nothing needs it in
between. The rule below makes no distinction between fixed and unfixed nodes: it removes any
non-quantity that nothing needs any more. Fixed nodes are the ones that matter for growth, and the
only ones that would otherwise stay forever.

## Freeing

The nodes to free are those that lead to nothing that remains. Let $F$ be the largest set of nodes
of $M'$ such that every $n \in F$

- is neither a quantity nor in $K(M')$,
- is a candidate or an ancestor of one, and
- has every consumer in $F$:

$$
	F = \bigcup \bigl\{\, X \subseteq \operatorname{anc}^*(C) \setminus (Q \cup K(M')) : \operatorname{cons}(X) \subseteq X \,\bigr\}, @tag(freed)
$$

where $Q$ is the quantities, $\operatorname{anc}^*(C)$ is the candidates and their ancestors in
$M'$, and $\operatorname{cons}(X)$ is the consumers of the nodes of $X$ in $M'$. A union of sets
closed under consumers is closed under consumers, so the largest such set exists and is their
union. Quantities are never freed: whether a quantity is in the model is the model's meaning, and
only a retirement takes one out.

The restriction to $\operatorname{anc}^*(C)$ is what makes the rule local. A node the user made
and never used, such as a mapped value computed from a surviving quantity to be read as a query's
target, has no consumer. Without the restriction it would satisfy the closure trivially and be
freed by a retirement that never touched it. With the restriction, a retirement frees only what
it has itself left unused.

<Statement {...freeing} />

<Proof>

(1) Let $n \in F$ and let $u$ be reached from $n$, along a path $n = u_0, u_1, \dots, u_k = u$.
Each $u_{i+1}$ is a consumer of $u_i$, so if $u_i \in F$ then $u_{i+1} \in F$, since $F$ is
closed under consumers. By induction $u \in F$. A value of a node outside $F$ is a function of its
ancestors alone, and none of them is in $F$, so it is unchanged when $F$ is removed. Every term,
binding and proposal that remains is in $K(M')$, so it is outside $F$.

(2) Let $n \in M' \setminus F$ have no consumer in $M'$, and suppose it had a consumer before the
retirement, and is not a quantity and not in $K(M')$. Its consumers in $M$ were all removed, so
they are in $R$ and $n \in \operatorname{in}(R) \setminus R \subseteq C$. Then $\{n\}$ is a subset
of $\operatorname{anc}^*(C) \setminus (Q \cup K(M'))$ whose consumers, there being none, lie in
it, so $n \in F$, a contradiction. A node of $S$ with no consumer is a candidate in the same way.

(3) The compilation graph is acyclic, so it has a topological order. Run reference counting from
the candidates. A node is visited only when it is a candidate or the input of a removed node, so
everything it removes lies in $\operatorname{anc}^*(C)$. It removes a node only when that node is
not a quantity, not in $K(M')$, and has all its consumers removed already. So the set it removes
is closed under consumers and is contained in $F$. Conversely, suppose some node of $F$ is never
removed, and take the latest such node $n$ in the topological order. Every consumer of $n$ is in
$F$ and later than $n$, so each was removed. When the last of them was removed, $n$ was visited as
its input, or $n$ was a candidate with no consumer to wait for. Then $n$ had no consumer left and
was removed, a contradiction. Acyclicity is what this step uses: on a graph with a cycle, the
nodes of an unused cycle each keep a consumer, and reference counting never frees them.

</Proof>

Part (1) is what makes freeing safe: no query, proposal or role that remains can tell the
difference. A program is compiled from the ancestors of its targets, so every program the new
model can compile is the one it compiled before freeing, as long as no target is in $F$. Part (2)
is what stops the growth. After any number of retirements, every node of the model is a quantity,
or an ancestor of something kept, or an ancestor of a node the user made and left unused. In a
sliding window whose ticks the user builds entirely from terms, the model's size is therefore
bounded by what its current quantities, terms, proposals and pins need. It no longer grows with
the number of ticks retired.

Freeing happens after the replacement is added, against $M'$. A caller may build the replacement
from a node that a retired term used, such as a scale shared by the retired terms and the factor
that replaces them. That node then has a consumer in $M'$ and stays. Freeing against $M$ would
remove it from under the replacement.

## An example

Take the core's test chain,

$$
	\randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(a, s^2), \quad
	\bfz_0 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, r^2), \quad
	\randomPoint_1 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, q^2), \quad
	\bfz_1 \mid \randomPoint_1 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_1, r^2),
$$

with constants $a$, $s$, $r$ and $q$. Each normal's log-density reads its scale through folded
values: its square, its logarithm. Each normal also binds the scale to its role. Retiring
$E = \{\randomPoint_0, \bfz_0\}$ removes the terms of $\randomPoint_0$, $\bfz_0$ and
$\randomPoint_1$, and every mapped value reached from $\randomPoint_0$ or $\bfz_0$. Then:

- $a$ and $s$, with the values folded from $s$, fed only the term of $\randomPoint_0$. They are
  candidates with no consumer left, and are freed.
- $q$ and its folded values fed only the term of $\randomPoint_1$, and are freed. The
  replacement [](eq:archive/core/math/kalman) is written with new constants, the variance
  $P + q^2$ among them. If the caller instead built $P + q^2$ from the node $q$ in the
  retirement's scope, $q$ would have a consumer in $M'$ and would stay.
- $r$, if one node serves both measurement terms, still feeds the term of $\bfz_1$, and stays. If
  each term was given its own constant, the one the term of $\bfz_0$ read is freed.
- Any value computed in the retirement's scope and not reached by the replacement, such as an
  intermediate the caller computed and did not use, is freed.

Extend the chain by a step and retire $\randomPoint_1$: the replacement just added is in
$\mathcal T_E$, and its constants are freed in turn. This is the pattern of a sliding window, and
the reason its node count stops growing.

## Handles the user holds

A handle is an identifier, cheap to copy and bound to no writer. The model cannot tell which
handles a user still holds. Identifiers are never reused, so a handle to a freed node never names
another node: using it fails as a handle to an unknown node does, and the snapshot before the
retirement still holds the node for any reader of that snapshot. What remains is to decide which
nodes the user can keep alive, which is the choice of $P$ in $K(M')$.

The case that matters is a constant shared across ticks: a scale made once, before the loop, and
used by every tick's terms. If a loop adds the next tick before retiring the oldest, as the
`sliding_window` example does, the scale always has a consumer and is never freed. If a loop
retires before adding, the scale may lose its last consumer at a retirement and be freed. The next
tick's use of its handle then fails.

There are three choices:

1. **$P = \emptyset$.** A handle keeps nothing alive. A constant to be reused across retirements is
   made again when it is needed, which is cheap, or the loop adds before it retires. This is the
   least machinery, and it keeps the meaning of a handle as it is: an identifier, valid in the
   snapshots that hold its node.
2. **$P$ named by the user.** The user marks the nodes to keep, and only those survive a
   retirement that leaves them unused. The proposition holds as written. Pins are then part of the
   model's state, and a pin needs a way to be released, or a pinned constant leaks just as now.
3. **$P$ the nodes whose handles are alive.** Handles become counted references. The model then
   knows what is held, but a handle is no longer a plain identifier, and every handle in the core
   changes.

Choice 1 is the one recommended here, with the failure of a stale handle named clearly in its
error. The third changes the core's handles for a convenience that remaking a constant gives for
free. The second can be added later without changing the mathematics, since it only grows $P$.

> [!TODO]
> Which nodes does a retirement keep for the user: none ($P = \emptyset$), the nodes the user pins,
> or every node whose handle is alive?

## In skoro's terms

- The compilation graph is the core's, with each node's consumers read forward. The nodes the
  model holds outside its edges, $H(M)$, are the terms' log-densities, the nodes bound to their
  roles, and the proposals' inputs and log-densities.
- Freeing is part of a retirement, done in the same change as the removal of $R$ and the addition
  of the replacement, against the model after both. No reader sees a model partly freed.
- Quantities are never freed. Fixed nodes (constants and folded values) are freed when they lose
  their last use, and so are mapped values of surviving quantities that a retirement leaves
  unused.
- No derivative, query or generation changes, by [%full](statement:freeing): every program over
  the remaining model is compiled from the same nodes as before.
- The work is proportional to the candidates and the ancestors visited from them, not to the
  model, as the removal of $R$ already is.

## Open questions

The choice of $P$ is open, in [Handles the user holds](#handles-the-user-holds).
